ikes me about your films is is  actually that you don't give a lot of  cues as to how people are supposed to  perceive people the characters in your  films you there is that balance between  an impartial depiction of a sort of  honest and real event or real things  that have taken place and you've spoken  a lot about the word agenda and politics  I was just wondering how how does that  change from the process of writing your  screenplay to working with you were  saying  that you were thinking sort of with the  actors in mind that you're writing for  when you actually come to working with  them especially with things like Detroit  where you're working with young  african-american actors now how do they  sort of perceive the writing that you've  or the lines that you've given them and  do you feel like it does come across as  more of an agenda when you've when it's  interpreted by them or do you think that  I don't know that's about it well in the  case of Detroit I wasn't actually I  didn't really do any rewriting on set I  wasn't actually there was my time I was  sort of splitting my time between that  and another project by the time it was  filming so and that that was much it was  a little unusual for me because I'm zero  dark thirty in The Hurt Locker I was  kind of there every every day and it's  kind of like pretty involved in almost  every frame and and in the case of  Detroit I think I probably had a few  conversations with actors not every one  of them but just but not not not all  that much actually so it was that was a  more typical experience of handing a  script off and then not really having as  much influence on how it turned out but  in terms of the writing so I don't  really know how to answer that question  but in terms of the writing the  orientation of the writing I there was a  when I back when I used to do a lot of  prose writing there was a teacher I had  who talked about the point of view of  the writer to his subject and and one of  the things that he was encouraging was  to kind of look as a writer at the  subjects as if you were at eye level  with them and sort of on the same moral  plane which is which sort of stayed with  me and so that is that that is how I try  to do it and not every writer does that  there's a lot of writers that  if you really look at the work there  they're assuming a slightly elevated  position to their characters as if they  have slightly more knowledge of the way  the world works and their characters do  which is fine I mean it's something  against that but it's it's and that's a  particular approach and so I tried not  to do that and try to just know what my  characters might know and you know I  have no idea what what the actors  thought of that to be honest but that's  where I start it must be interesting  when you then watch it back to see how  your lines have been interpreted by  those people and I imagine it must be  sometimes surprising to see how it comes  across what you wrote one time and then  to see it performed do you ever feel  like wow that's given a new life to what  I sort of interpret or how I well yeah  completely I mean having your work  performed by actors is an amazing  experience it's actually probably the  best part of this job and we did a table  read of The Hurt Locker early on with  some friends and I was quite depressed  afterwards because I thought oh my god  the script is terrible and Catherine  said no no it's just it's these the  actors weren't really you know it's not  that there were anything wrong with them  but then and then when she cast Jeremy  and we did another reading I had the I  thought oh my god I'm a genius and so it  I mean it really does come down to  casting so much and a good actor can can  give almost anything life and so it's a  great great pleasure and a great  privilege to have your work performed as  opposed to just written and I mean it's  it's fun to write in everything but it's  but it's really it's an honor to see  so